00</pubDate><content:encoded><![CDATA[<img src="https://www.theglobeandmail.com/resizer/v2/QDQWKSROJRDRTDXK737XW6ZFBM.jpg?auth=688d5627917cf9248df09768e1fe284e1c1e68a6187a5d3e333ed26d0f5f7b51&smart=true&width=5979&height=3604" alt="The Canadian Forces Snowbirds perform at the Aero Gatineau-Ottawa air show in Gatineau, Que., Sept. 19." height="3604" width="5979"/>]]></content:encoded><media:content url="https://www.theglobeandmail.com/resizer/v2/QDQWKSROJRDRTDXK737XW6ZFBM.jpg?auth=688d5627917cf9248df09768e1fe284e1c1e68a6187a5d3e333ed26d0f5f7b51&amp;smart=true&amp;width=5979&amp;height=3604" type="image/jpeg" height="3604" width="5979"><media:description type="plain"><![CDATA[The Canadian Forces Snowbirds perform at the Aero Gatineau-Ottawa air show in Gatineau, Que., on Saturday, Sept. 19, 2026. THE CANADIAN PRESS/Patrick Doyle]]></media:description><media:credit role="author" scheme="urn:ebu">PATRICK DOYLE</media:credit></media:content></item><item><title><![CDATA[Business Brief: The future of corporate taxation]]></title><link>https://www.theglobeandmail.com/business/article-business-brief-the-future-of-corporate-taxation/</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.theglobeandmail.com/business/article-business-brief-the-future-of-corporate-taxation/</guid><dc:creator><![CDATA[REDACTED]]></dc:creator><description><![CDATA[Plus, low international student enrolment in Atlantic Canada and North America’s challenged auto industry ]]></description><pubDate>Fri, 16 Oct 2026 10:23:43 +0000</pubDate><content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>Good morning. Last month, Prime Minister Mark Carney unveiled the biggest corporate tax change in two decades: the productivity mega deduction, which allows companies to deduct the cost of many capital investments up front. The move has corporate Canada buzzing. But there are also calls for Ottawa to go further with a comprehensive reform of the corporate tax system. More on that below.</p>]]></content:encoded><media:content url="https://www.theglobeandmail.com/resizer/v2/FMOYPQ46RRBL3FV4KPG2GSETTY.JPG?auth=8fc1c03d3c09895815003d031df3910780781671ee10e31e3cee2e9b1b0e9418&amp;smart=true&amp;width=7136&amp;height=4760" type="image/jpeg" height="4760" width="7136"><media:description type="plain"><![CDATA[Prime Minister Mark Carney, right, shakes hands with Finance Minister François-Philippe Champagne before the tabling of this year's spring economic update.]]></media:description><media:credit role="author" scheme="urn:ebu">Justin Tang</media:credit></media:content></item></channel></rss>
